WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.633 --> 00:00:10.200
So, my name is Erling Norrby.

00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:12.633
I have a background in the field of medicine.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.833
I'm also a Professor of Virology at the Karolinska Institute for 25 years.

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:21.033
And then I moved to the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:23.433
where I serve as the so-called Permanent Secretary,

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:27.933
so I have the special title of being the former Permanent Secretary.

00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:31.400
I had my office at the Center for the Institute for Science,

00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:34.500
which tells you that I have become more and more interested

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:41.067
in how does science evolve. And in studying that, I've taken advantage

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.800
of absolutely unique material that we have

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:46.433
at both the Karolinska Institute and at the Royal Swedish Academy

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:50.333
of Sciences, and these are the archives of the Nobel prizes.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:56.133
Now the Nobel prizes have been distributed for more than a hundred years.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:01:00.933
And it's by far the most prestigious prize that there is in science.

00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:06.733
And the reason for that is that one has developed a very efficient technique

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.200
to select Nobel prize laureates.

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:14.100
And what I will tell you first will be how do we go about it,

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:16.567
but maybe we should start actually

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:23.000
with the will that Alfred Nobel  wrote in 1895.

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:25.833
and I have just extracted a part of that will

00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:30.500
in order to emphasize some of the specifications that he gave.

00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:33.600
And you can see from this text here

00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:37.867
that there should be... one prize should be given in the field of physics.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:45.300
And that should be for the most important discovery or invention.

00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.067
And then there should be another prize in chemistry,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.533
and that should be for a discovery or an improvement.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:57.200
And the third prize in natural sciences is to physiology or medicine

00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:00.467
and that can only be given for a discovery.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.267
So we have to reflect very carefully on what is a discovery, and that is an ongoing debate

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.600
even a hundred years after the first prizes.

00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:16.333
In Nobel's will, it specified who is going to be responsible

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.433
for selecting the prize recipients.

00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:22.667
The so-called Nobel Institutes.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:28.033
So there are four bodies that are responsible for selecting the prize recipients.

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:29.467
Those are the prize-giving institutions.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.033
The first institution is the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences,

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:38.433
which is responsible for both the prize in Physics and in Chemistry.

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:45.267
And then the Karolinska Institute is responsible for the prize in Physiology or Medicine.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:51.267
And the Swedish Academy of Literature is responsible for the prize in Literature.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.533
And there is also a Peace prize, and that is given by a committee

00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:59.400
selected by the House of Parliament in Norway.

00:02:59.433 --> 00:03:01.767
And it's a 5 member committee.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.233
In order to implement Nobel's will,

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:10.233
which actually took quite some time. He died in 1896,

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:14.933
and it wasn't until 1900 that one could start giving Nobel prizes.

00:03:14.967 --> 00:03:19.600
The reason was there was not a formal body that could receive the money

00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:20.733
or take care of the money.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:25.167
And therefore one created something called the Nobel Foundation.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.000
The Nobel Foundation is not actually in the will,

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.533
but it serves a very important, or several important functions

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:35.333
for the prize giving institutions.

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:37.800
One, it is of course to take care of the money

00:03:37.833 --> 00:03:44.667
and secure that the money is well cared for and that one can keep the prize at a good level.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.000
The other is for legal aspects.

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:53.067
So the Nobel Foundation is responsible for making sure that the legality

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.000
of the way the work is done is correct.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:04:01.167
The third is that for practical reason the Nobel Foundation arranges the prize ceremony,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.967
in Stockholm which includes all prize recipients except the Peace prize of course,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.267
which has its own ceremony in Oslo.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:15.733
So these are the bodies, and how do these different bodies go about selecting the members.

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:19.733
In order to understand that, we need to look at something I call the Nobel Year.

00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:28.167
And this is a comprehensive process that has evolved over many, many years,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.100
but there are certain absolute specifications that need to be addressed.

00:04:32.133 --> 00:04:34.433
The first is that in order to be considered for a Nobel prize,

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:36.400
you have to be nominated for the prize.

00:04:36.433 --> 00:04:43.033
And only invited people can nominate someone for the prize.

00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:48.300
And if you see in this clock the yellow blurbs that we call them,

00:04:48.333 --> 00:04:54.267
they illustrate that first we select who is going to invite to give...

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.433
to potentially give a recommendation for a prize,

00:04:57.467 --> 00:05:01.133
and then this is sent out.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:08.167
And the red circle here illustrates that the nominations should be in before January 31st

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.000
in the year when the prize is going to be given.

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:18.133
And over all these years, of course there have been variations in the number of invitations sent out,

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:22.733
and the number of nominations, but at the present time,

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:30.333
we send out invitations to nominate to more than two to three thousand individuals or organizations,

00:05:30.367 --> 00:05:35.600
and we get overall three, four, sometimes even five hundred nominations,

00:05:35.633 --> 00:05:38.133
so it is a huge material to manage.

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:45.300
Once all the nominations are in, then of course the Nobel Committee starts to do its work.

00:05:45.333 --> 00:05:49.867
What the committee... Nobel committees have sat down almost since the very beginning

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:57.367
is to make in depth reviews, very careful reviews of a certain proposal.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:03.967
and then reviews are prepared during the summer, and in August each year,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.200
then the committee compiles all the different reviews.

00:06:10.233 --> 00:06:16.900
and starts to really make further prioritization to see which are the real runner's up.

00:06:16.933 --> 00:06:21.267
And in September this discussion goes on

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.967
and hopefully then the committee comes to an agreement that this year we would like

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.433
to propose this particular candidate.

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:33.100
Most often this candidate had been discussed for many years before that.

00:06:33.133 --> 00:06:39.933
The whole process... it is important that one can consolidate this and one can make repeated reviews.

00:06:39.967 --> 00:06:43.800
So what is in the Nobel Archives that make it so interesting?

00:06:43.833 --> 00:06:48.633
That is first of all, nominations, and they can be in many different languages.

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:53.233
Could be in Spanish, or in French or in German or whatever.

00:06:53.267 --> 00:07:02.800
And then the reviews are in Swedish, and this material is a unique, real time evaluation of science.

00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:05.700
The way one looked at science at that particular time.

00:07:05.733 --> 00:07:12.000
And then there is one more thing to the archives, and that is when I started to understand

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:13.733
that they were interesting material.

00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:17.300
One decided that there should be fifty year secrecy.

00:07:17.333 --> 00:07:23.000
So we are not allowed to look at the material until fifty years pass.

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:25.700
But after that, they are available for scholarly work.

00:07:25.733 --> 00:07:30.900
It's not for journalists to go in there just out of curiosity, but for scholarly work.

00:07:30.933 --> 00:07:35.667
And I have come to be involved in using this unique material

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:38.433
and the more I work with this material,

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:44.000
the more impressed I have been of what a unique kind of material it is.

00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:54.033
It started way back in 2005. It so happened that Dr. Stan Prusiner here at UCSF

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:57.367
who has his own Nobel Prize for his discovery of prions,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:04.233
he and I were both interested in understanding the Nobel prize of 1954

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:07.200
to Thomas Weller, Frederick Robbins and John Enders.

00:08:07.233 --> 00:08:09.700
And this is a very important Nobel Prize.

00:08:09.733 --> 00:08:14.967
They would show that poliovirus can grow in any kind of cells.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:22.467
And before that the dogma was that poliovirus can only replicate in nerve cells.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:28.167
And because they could show this, all of a sudden it became possible to make a vaccine against polio.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:35.800
And that was a most dramatic achievement because during the first half of the 20th century

00:08:35.833 --> 00:08:44.367
polio was the number one disease that was a scare in society, and that is forgotten today

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.600
because we have been so successful in vaccinating and removing the disease.

00:08:48.633 --> 00:08:52.367
But the question here was why did the Nobel committee rush it?

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:56.300
Why didn't they wait and include Jonas Salk in the prize?

00:08:56.333 --> 00:09:04.300
We could really get some insight into that and could write a rather interesting evaluation

00:09:04.333 --> 00:09:08.133
of the way one discussed this in the Nobel committee.

00:09:08.167 --> 00:09:14.200
And we could publish this in a regular scientific journal.

00:09:14.233 --> 00:09:19.600
At that stage I realized that maybe one could actually turn it into a book,

00:09:19.633 --> 00:09:25.233
and that led to that in 2010, I published a book on Nobel Prizes and Life Sciences.

00:09:25.267 --> 00:09:30.867
So it includes both the background material about Alfred Nobel,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:38.733
about how the will came about, about how... the formal aspects of the prize.

00:09:38.767 --> 00:09:44.033
It discusses phenomena like serendipity and then the field that I mention there

00:09:44.067 --> 00:09:53.633
It also discusses nucleic acids, which are of course a very central field when it comes to changes in attitude.

00:09:53.667 --> 00:09:56.867
And I call that chapter, "A Drama in Five Acts".

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:01.100
And I am not going to go into any detail on that,

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:05.833
but just to mention that a part of this was a very careful evaluation of Oswald Avery

00:10:05.867 --> 00:10:13.133
and his... the discussion about him in the Nobel Prize context.

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:16.367
This is a person who should have received a Nobel Prize, but who never did.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:18.833
And there are several reasons for that.

00:10:18.867 --> 00:10:27.967
He was way ahead of his time when he in 1944 published that if you extracted DNA out of Pneumococcus,

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.000
you could change the properties of other Pneumococcus by simply transferring DNA.

00:10:32.033 --> 00:10:35.200
And it was not believed by the scientific community,

00:10:35.233 --> 00:10:41.300
and a lot of antagonists even in his own institution at the Rockefeller Institute as it was called,

00:10:41.333 --> 00:10:42.567
and then later on Rockefeller University.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:51.133
But he was discussed carefully. In fact the year after he died there was a posthumous nomination of him

00:10:51.167 --> 00:10:54.200
and that was evaluated, for a prize in Chemistry,

00:10:54.233 --> 00:10:57.867
that was evaluated by the Swedish Nobel laureate Arne Tiselius,

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.133
and he said that had this material been available

00:11:01.167 --> 00:11:03.600
before, and had he been alive, he should have had a Nobel prize.

00:11:03.633 --> 00:11:11.300
So this is an example of a name that should have been in the list of Nobel laureates, but it's not there.

00:11:11.333 --> 00:11:19.333
Now inspired by this, in the first book I continued to  review the archives.

00:11:19.367 --> 00:11:22.367
Now this new book, which is 50% larger than the previous one,

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:28.100
it discusses mostly prizes between 1960 and 62.

00:11:28.133 --> 00:11:31.900
And these are very important years of course.

00:11:31.933 --> 00:11:40.300
There was the very important prize in immunology in 1960 for so-called induced tolerance.

00:11:40.333 --> 00:11:47.100
That is, that our immune system must be of the kind that it does not react against our own cells.

00:11:47.133 --> 00:11:52.167
And therefore during the embryonic development,

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:58.067
the immune system, the cells that can react with our own body are obliterated.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:03.800
And it's a very important mechanism. This is interesting to discuss, Burnet and Medawar,

00:12:03.833 --> 00:12:08.333
because Burnet was a virologist.  He was a dominating virologist of his generation.

00:12:08.367 --> 00:12:16.133
And the committee discussed this from 1948 and onward and said the Nobel prize should be given to Burnet.

00:12:16.167 --> 00:12:22.267
He is such an outstanding virologist, but couldn't really find a good discovery among all the things that he had discovered.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:30.700
And therefore, but Burnet was also a fantastic life science philosopher,

00:12:30.733 --> 00:12:34.800
and he speculated about the defense mechanism, the immune defense mechanism.

00:12:34.833 --> 00:12:39.733
And he postulated that such a phenomenon of tolerance must exist.

00:12:39.767 --> 00:12:44.600
And therefore in 1960, the Nobel committee, on its own initiative,

00:12:44.633 --> 00:12:47.767
combined Burnet who was a virologist,

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:51.500
but had postulated that you could induce this tolerance,

00:12:51.533 --> 00:12:56.267
and Medawar who had proven that in various experiments with his collaborators.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:01.867
These two, Peter Medawar and Macfarlane Burnet are two giants in science.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.800
Really statesmen of science.

00:13:03.833 --> 00:13:07.200
And they influenced science in many different ways,

00:13:07.233 --> 00:13:11.400
not the least through the fantastic books that they have written.

00:13:11.433 --> 00:13:16.733
And I wanted to share with you this picture because it is quite a historical picture.

00:13:16.767 --> 00:13:20.900
The man in the middle here is Lawrence Bragg.

00:13:20.933 --> 00:13:23.833
He is the youngest Nobel laureate ever.

00:13:23.867 --> 00:13:31.900
He got the Nobel prize, together with his father when he was 25 years old.

00:13:31.933 --> 00:13:34.967
And in Medicine the youngest one is actually 32 years.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.633
But what makes this picture unique is that this was taken in 1943,

00:13:40.667 --> 00:13:43.267
in the middle of World War II.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:46.433
Bragg was representing something called the British Council,

00:13:46.467 --> 00:13:51.233
and that was to further cultural contacts between the different countries in Europe.

00:13:51.267 --> 00:13:55.867
But there wasn't very many countries that he could contact, but Sweden was one of them

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:59.200
because Sweden managed to stay out of the Second World War.

00:13:59.233 --> 00:14:04.567
So here Bragg flies in to Stockholm and visits Uppsala

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:07.500
and the people you see if you look from the left to the right are

00:14:07.533 --> 00:14:14.500
all these major actors, The Svedberg, the Nobel laureate in 1926,

00:14:14.533 --> 00:14:17.633
Arne Fredga, who was a member for a long, long time,

00:14:17.667 --> 00:14:19.500
Arne Tiselius, the Nobel laureate, 1948,

00:14:19.533 --> 00:14:28.033
and Gunnar Hagg to the right of Bragg, and then there's also a physicist Axel Lindh, who was there.

00:14:28.067 --> 00:14:34.167
But it's nice to reflect on that fact that this is just 1943.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:39.233
Now Bragg is a very, very central person to our story.

00:14:39.267 --> 00:14:44.500
And the story has to do with what happened in 1962.

00:14:44.533 --> 00:14:48.900
But then, as you probably all know, in 1953

00:14:48.933 --> 00:14:54.800
Watson and Crick published the double helix structure of DNA.

00:14:54.833 --> 00:14:58.400
And that was published on April 28th that year.

00:14:58.433 --> 00:15:04.300
One would think then that this is the number one discovery of the previous century.

00:15:04.333 --> 00:15:08.600
That this would be immediately taken up by the scientific community,

00:15:08.633 --> 00:15:13.167
processed and really interpreted, and integrated in the science of the day.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:21.167
But the step that was to be taken, namely to conclude that DNA is the information carrying molecule

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:23.833
was a very major step and it took time.

00:15:23.867 --> 00:15:32.633
And this is reflected in a quite surprising phenomenon, namely that it took until 1960

00:15:32.667 --> 00:15:35.233
before there was even a nomination for a prize

00:15:35.267 --> 00:15:40.067
to Watson and Crick. And the one person who took the initiative of that was Bragg.

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:47.533
It was Bragg who was responsible for the Cavendish Laboratory and he knew both what was going on

00:15:47.567 --> 00:15:52.433
in the Watson and Crick discovery, but also the protein stuff that was done in Cavendish.

00:15:52.467 --> 00:15:59.500
And to be tactical, the way Bragg did this, he nominated the protein crystallographer, Perutz,

00:15:59.533 --> 00:16:05.467
and Kendrew and actually also another crystallographer, Hodgkin, for a prize in Physics.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:12.167
And then he nominated Watson and Crick, and since he knew what a complicated story,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:15.867
he also included Wilkins. And so they were nominated for a prize in Chemistry.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:21.633
The Physics committee immediately said no, this is not our responsibility,

00:16:21.667 --> 00:16:25.900
so the Chemistry committee have to consider both these nominations.

00:16:25.933 --> 00:16:33.633
And that led to long discussions and followed up through the years, you can see the number of nominations here.

00:16:33.667 --> 00:16:36.167
Notice that most of the nominations are for Watson and Crick.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:38.467
But some nominations also include Wilkins,

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:42.867
and that is because of a long story in itself.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:48.600
So, the prizes were awarded in 1962, and Wilkins was included.

00:16:48.633 --> 00:16:51.233
Here you can see Crick receiving the prize.

00:16:51.267 --> 00:16:55.367
Watson stands behind him, and Wilkins further back.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:58.100
And to the left are Perutz and Kendrew.

00:16:58.133 --> 00:17:07.967
This was a year of scientific giants, and a lot can be said about these particular prizes.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:14.333
It is striking that when these three recipients, Crick, Watson and Wilkins were going to give their Nobel lectures

00:17:14.367 --> 00:17:18.767
Crick certainly did not talk about the structure of DNA.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:20.333
He talked about the genetic code

00:17:20.367 --> 00:17:25.967
because immense things had happened in these years between '53 and '62.

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:31.200
Watson spoke about the importance of RNA, not about the DNA double helix.

00:17:31.233 --> 00:17:35.300
Wilkins was the only one that talked about the structure of DNA.

00:17:35.333 --> 00:17:40.233
Jim Watson when he received the Nobel prize,

00:17:40.267 --> 00:17:50.167
was 34 years old and he is the third youngest among those who received the prize in physiology or medicine.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:55.467
The youngest is Banting who received the prize for discovering insulin.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:58.967
And then Josh Lederberg was 33.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:03.733
But Watson was 34, and being in good health,

00:18:03.767 --> 00:18:07.833
he could re-visit Sweden in 2012

00:18:07.867 --> 00:18:14.533
to celebrate his 50 year anniversary of receiving the Nobel prize.

00:18:14.567 --> 00:18:19.900
And here he is on stage with Matt Ridley, who is interviewing him and asking him

00:18:19.933 --> 00:18:26.767
what happened in the morning of February 28, 1953

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:31.567
when you were the first one who understood the structure of DNA.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.867
And that is history alive.

